The detection circuit is three flip-flops clocked on the rising edge of clk:
  s1 <= irq_a; s2 <= s1; s3 <= s2;
  irq_pulse = s2 & ~s3;

Timing data:
- Every flip-flop has setup time tsu = 0.3 ns and hold time th = 0.2 ns.
- Ignore clock-to-q delay, wire delay and clock skew.
- If s1 samples during a setup/hold violation, it goes metastable, but it resolves to a valid 0 or 1 before the next clock edge. You cannot know which value it resolves to.

Answer:
(a) the minimum W, in ns, that guarantees irq_pulse fires exactly once for every input pulse, whatever the phase between irq_a and clk;
(b) the input alignment that sets this worst case;
(c) whether a 3 ns pulse can be detected, and whether it can be missed;
(d) at which clock edge irq_pulse rises and how long it stays high. Count from edge k, the first edge at which s1 captures a 1.

